## Read first
- `{{gate_dir}}/data/scope.json`: the theorem's exact dependency closure in the library (every member with its module, file, keyword line, kind, depth from the theorem and proof status); `informal/declarations.json`: each member's elaborated one-line signature and its line range.
- The library's source under `{{library_src}}/`, the files of the scope's members: their docstrings, module docstrings and statements.

## Choose the mathematical narrative
The dependency closure is a checking inventory, not the outline. Select constructions,
substantial lemmas and deductions that explain the theorem. Keep implementation helpers
traceable without assigning each a numbered statement: casts, restriction followed by
inclusion, pointwise formulas and immediate consequences normally stay implicit. Give each
section a mathematical job; fold notation-only preliminaries into the arguments that need
them. Put specialized machinery near its first substantial use. Record the reason for each
omission in items-review.md rather than adding exposition to justify the inventory.

## Write
1. `informal/items.json`, in this shape: `schema` `formal-companion/informalize-items`, `schema_version` 1; `library` with `commit` `{{library_commit}}` and `declarations_sha256` `{{declarations_sha256}}` (the sha256 of `informal/declarations.json` as it is); `reference_work` {{#if reference_work}}`"{{reference_work}}"`{{/if}}{{#unless reference_work}}`null`{{/unless}}; `sections` (`number` "1", "2", ... as strings, `title` in LaTeX source, `slug` lowercase words joined by `-`); `items`; `deferred` `[]` and `decisions` `[]`. Each item: `id` `<section>.<n>` (provisional: unique, in the section it names, gaps allowed; the maintainer's approval renumbers them), `section`, `kind` (definition, lemma, proposition, theorem, corollary, example or remark), `title` (LaTeX source, as the text will print it), `gist` (one sentence in your own words of what the Lean states), `parts` (`[{letter, gist, reference}]`, letters a, b, ... in order, or `[]`), `in_v1` (true for what version 1 states), `reference`, `departures` (why a key declaration is not an exact match, else ""), and `declarations`: `{name, module, line, part, role, relation, junk, hypotheses}` with `line` the declaration's keyword line as `informal/declarations.json` gives it, `role` `key` (it states the item or the part) or `helper`, `relation` `exactMatch`, `broadMatch`, `narrowMatch` or `relatedMatch`, `junk` the junk values the statement relies on in words, and `hypotheses` the binders of its signature the statement must carry -- the ones that state mathematics, not the typeclass, universe and index binders that carry the ambient structure, which the conventions section fixes once. Every key declaration of an item with parts names its part; every part of an `in_v1` item has a key declaration, and so does the item.
2. `informal/items-review.md`, for the maintainer: one row per item (id, kind, title, the key declarations per part, the reference number and its source, departures), then why each scope member that is no key declaration was left out.

## Reference numbers
`reference` is `{number, source, declaration}` or null, on the item or on a part when the number covers only that part. `number` as the reference work prints it (`Theorem 1.20`), never a quotation; `source` is `docstring` (a docstring of `declaration` states it), `module docstring`, `inferred` (from the work's order; printed with a dagger) or `none`. Never guess a number you cannot ground in the library's text.

